// The write-after-read policy AS THE DOGWOOD GUIDE PRINTS IT -- `04-temporal-expressions.md` and
// its runnable bundle `dogwood-docs/examples/write_after_read_formerly/policy.dw`, at c6237c88 --
// verbatim below, deployed beside the same read access control as skill-set.dw.
// Dogwood is (c) Amazon.com Inc, Apache 2.0. See README.md.
//
// The ONE difference from skill-set.dw is the event kind: `Read::response`, which the guide glosses
// as "successfully read", where the skill has `Read::request`.

// -- ours: who may read. Anyone may read any document except the restricted one.
permit ( principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Read", resource );

forbid ( principal, action == Drupe::Action::"Read", resource )
when { context.input.document == "restricted" };

// -- the guide's example, verbatim
// Permit a Write only if the same user successfully read the same document
// within the last hour (the canonical write-after-read policy). `formerly
// within 1h` is the existential past operator; `input.user`/`input.document`
// pin the past Read to the CURRENT request via context.* correlation.
@id("write_after_read")
permit (
    principal,
    action == Drupe::Action::"Write",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Drupe::Action::"Read"::response{
        input.user: context.input.user,
        input.document: context.input.document
    }
};
